{"name":"Foggy Dew","hex":"#d1d5d0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foggy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Obscured by mist or fog; unclear; hazy.","Confused, befuddled, or vague."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foggy"},{"term":"dew","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any moisture from the atmosphere condensed by cool bodies upon their surfaces.","Moisture in the air that settles on plants, etc in the morning or evening, resulting in drops."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To wet with, or as if with, dew; to moisten.","To deposit dew."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Foggy Dew (English song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Foggy Dew\" or \"Foggy, Foggy Dew\" is an English folk song with a strong presence in the South of England and the Southern United States in the nineteenth century. The song describes the outcome of an affair between a weaver and a girl he courted. It is cataloged as Laws No. O03 and Roud Folk Song Index No. 558. It has been recorded by many traditional singers including Harry Cox, and a diverse range of musicians including Benjamin Britten, Burl Ives, A. L. Lloyd and Ye Vagabonds have arranged and recorded popular versions of the song."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foggy_Dew_(English_song)"},{"title":"The Foggy Dew (Irish songs)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Foggy Dew\" is the name of several Irish ballads, and of an Irish lament. The most popular song of that name chronicles the Easter Rising of 1916, and encourages Irishmen to fight for the cause of Ireland, rather than for the British Empire, as so many young men were doing in World War I."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Foggy_Dew_(Irish_songs)"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog"},{"title":"Dew","blocks":[{"text":"Dew is water in the form of droplets that appears on thin, exposed objects in the morning or evening due to condensation. \nAs the exposed surface cools by radiating its heat, atmospheric moisture condenses at a rate greater than that at which it can evaporate, resulting in the formation of water droplets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/A_drop_of_dew_on_a_clover.jpg/330px-A_drop_of_dew_on_a_clover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dew"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Minute droplets of water constitute this after-dark radiation fog, with an ambient temperature of −2 °C (28 °F). Their motion trails are captured as streaks.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of water droplets forming fog. Those outside the camera lens's depth of field appear as orbs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/1280px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Ground fog in Frankfurt, Germany.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/500px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/1280px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/500px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advection fog layer in San Francisco with the Golden Gate Bridge and skyline in the background",